WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.400
Inside they very large eyes of a squid

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.840
are spherical lenses.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.680
Usually, spherical lenses can’t bend the light

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.000
that passes through them enough for

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.760
the image to look focused,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.660
but these cephalopods found a way around that.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.920
Their lenses are built up of proteins

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.760
called S-crystallins.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.900
Two loops on the surface of this protein

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.160
allow it to self-associate into a gel.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.020
Slight variations in the protein

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.820
changes the loops and how they link.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.780
The types of links vary in a gradient along the lens –

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.980
denser in the middle and sparser at the edges

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.720
This gradient arises during development.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.040
At the larval stage,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.920
the proteins are packed together in bundles of 6 —

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.160
forming the dense gel core.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.760
When particles can no longer

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.620
diffuse easily through the core,

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.480
the proteins form a layer with fewer linked

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.500
S-crystallins on the outside of the core.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.700
This continues until the outermost layer,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.720
made with just pairs of proteins, completes the lens.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.760
This change in density across the lens

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.900
allows light that passes through the center of the

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.940
lens to bend differently than light that falls on the edge,

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.640
thus focusing what would’ve been a blurry image.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.500
The S-crystallins in squids’ lenses

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.200
act like “patchy colloids.”

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.180
These are groups of molecules that exert

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.220
attractive forces on one another and can form complex

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.960
shapes out of repeated units,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.340
just like the bunches of S-crystallin proteins.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.460
With these findings and further research,

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.400
scientists think squid lenses could eventually

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.280
be replicated with simpler materials

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.740
and used in optical instruments and clinics.

